 abstract = {The face recognition systems are susceptible to presentation attacks, where faces are presented in front of cameras via mediums such as photos, videos, or masks to deceive the face recognition systems. Due to the high similarity between 3D masks and real faces, detecting such attacks remains challenging. To enhance the robustness and generalization ability of 3D mask attack detection, we propose a dual-stream 3D mask presentation attack detection method based on physiological signals and deceptive textures. Firstly, spatiotemporal convolutional neural networks are utilized to extract relationships between skin tone changes and pulses from facial videos, along with the design of an image phase-driven attention mechanism to suppress interference from head movements. Secondly, multiscale central difference convolutional networks are employed to supplement the feature differences between real faces and 3D mask attacks from the perspective of texture disparity. Finally, accurate differentiation between genuine and fake faces is achieved through the classification results of the combined features from both streams. This method integrates physiological information from real faces and texture information from attack mask faces, addressing the susceptibility of methods based on physiological signals to lighting changes and head movements. The results of experiment on multiple datasets validate the effectualness and feasibility of this method in detecting 3D mask attacks. The codes are available at https://github.com/YuXiaolu801/DSTRN. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2024.},
 author = {Sun, Rui and Yu, Xiaolu and Feng, Huidong and Wang, Fei and Zhang, Xudong},
